# G069 — WYSIWYG Editor

*v2. See `wysiwyg.md` for prose: the model IS the render (no source
form), runs as right granularity, range operations reshape runs,
attributes compose as sets, rendering is a trivial projection.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Note Editor with Live Preview

[vault-note-editor @note-path
    IN ORDER TO
        edit a note in runs-of-attributed-text form
        where the model the user mutates is the model the renderer walks
        so there is no source-to-preview round-trip to keep in sync

    @doc <- [wysiwyg[new]]
    [wysiwyg[insert
              {doc: @doc, pos: 0,
               text: [vault[read-runs {path: @note-path}]], attrs: []}]]

    sync @user-events {
        bold-selection @start @end ->
            [wysiwyg[apply-style
                      {doc: @doc, start: @start, end: @end,
                       attr: "bold"}]]
            [render[live-preview
                     {html: [wysiwyg[to-html {doc: @doc}]]}]]
            [vault[persist {path: @note-path, runs: @doc:runs}]]
    }
]

---

## Verb Surface

[wysiwyg
    new
    insert           {doc, pos, text, attrs}
    delete-range     {doc, start, end}
    apply-style      {doc, start, end, attr}
    remove-style     {doc, start, end, attr}
    split-at         {doc, pos}
    normalize        {doc}
    to-html          {doc}
    to-plain         {doc}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: runs stay canonical `where` invariants hold

[wysiwyg[normalize {doc: @doc}]
    IN ORDER TO
        restore the three run invariants after every mutation
        so the storage never drifts into pathological states

    @doc:runs
        where run text is empty        -> drop
        where two adjacent runs share attrs -> merge
        where final concatenation     -> equal to @doc plain text
]

## Distinctive Innate idiom: style applies `where` range intersects run

[wysiwyg[apply-style {doc: @doc, start: @s, end: @e, attr: @a}]
    split runs at both @s and @e
    then for each run entirely inside [@s, @e]
        where @a is in run:attrs   -> leave alone
        otherwise                   -> add @a to run:attrs (set union)
    then normalize
]

Attributes compose as set union; bold applied to bold-italic keeps
italic. Every projection (HTML, plain, markdown) is a 20-line walk
because the model did the hard work.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @doc <- [wysiwyg[new]]
    [wysiwyg[insert
              {doc: @doc, pos: 0,
               text: "Hello world", attrs: []}]]
    [wysiwyg[apply-style
              {doc: @doc, start: 0, end: 5, attr: "bold"}]]
    [wysiwyg[apply-style
              {doc: @doc, start: 6, end: 11, attr: "italic"}]]

    -> [wysiwyg[to-html {doc: @doc}]]
    ;; -> "<b>Hello</b> <i>world</i>"

    -> [wysiwyg[to-plain {doc: @doc}]]
    ;; -> "Hello world"
]
